 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.1,
   "dur": 4.261,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "How long to fall through the Earth? Step in, and let go."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 5.1,
   "dur": 7.922,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "The tunnel runs straight through the middle of the planet, so long that driving it would take days. But you'll be falling."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 14.1,
   "dur": 11.504,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "And gravity keeps pulling, until at the center you're moving about ten kilometers a second. Then that pull slows you on the way up, and you arrive just as you stop."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 26.7,
   "dur": 13.676,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "That whole trip, the textbooks said, takes forty-two minutes. But they pictured the same stuff all the way down. A graduate student redid the sum with the real Earth, and its heavy core makes gravity grow stronger as you fall."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 42.9,
   "dur": 8.767,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "So you fall faster, sooner, and come out the other side in about thirty-eight minutes. With nobody to catch you, you'd swing back through, forever."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 55.5,
   "dur": 4.757,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "Lighten that core in the sum, and the answer drifts back toward the textbook's."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 61.5,
   "dur": 6.354,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "So a fall through the Earth is more than a ride. It's a way of weighing its heart."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 68.7,
   "dur": 3.721,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next envelope: could everyone alive stand in one city?"
  }
 ]
}